Fluid HTN is a forward decomposition planner that uses partial planning and domain splicing. It includes a Domain Builder for easy design of HTN domains, and supports run-time splicing through domain slots. Replanning only occurs when plans complete/fail or when the world state changes, with early rejection of replanning that is unlikely to succeed. It can be easily extended using the extension library, and uses a Factory interface for memory management. Decomposition logging is also available for debugging purposes.
Compound Tasks
Compound tasks are the basis of HTN's hierarchical structure. They consist of high-level tasks that can be accomplished in multiple ways. There are two types: Selectors and Sequencers. Selectors break down a single sub-task, while Sequencers break down all sub-tasks for success. Additional task types like RandomSelect and UtilitySelect can be added. These tasks are broken down until only Primitive Tasks remain, which make up the final plan. Compound tasks include subtasks and conditions.
Primitive Tasks
Primitive tasks represent a single step that can be performed by our AI. A set of primitive tasks is the plan that we are ultimately getting out of the HTN. Primitive tasks are comprised of an operator, a set of effects, a set of conditions and a set of executing conditions.
Conditions
Conditions are boolean validators that can be used to validate the decomposition of a compound task, or the validity of a primitive task. Primitive Tasks also have Executing Conditions, which we validate before every update to the primary task's operator during execution of a plan.
Executing Conditions
Advanced: Executing Conditions are evaluated every planner tick and are useful when you need to re-evaluate the validity of your conditions after planning or setting a new task as the current task during execution. If the operator returns "continue" and the condition becomes invalid, the planner may not automatically know. Using Executing Conditions or having the logic in the Operator return "Failure" can both trigger a replan and ensure that the planner recognizes the task is invalid.
Operators
Operators are the logic operation a primitive task should perform during plan execution. Every time an operator updates, it returns a status whether it succeeded, failed or need to continue next tick.
Effects
Effects change the world state during planning and execution, but only apply to primitive tasks. There are three types: PlanOnly effects are temporary and removed before execution; PlanAndExecute effects are re-applied after successful execution; and Permanent effects are applied during planning and persist during execution. Permanent effects are useful when changing state only during planning or when making changes in runtime code.
