To display real-time candles on the chart and calculate the percentage at the current price, you can use the Binance API to get the real-time data.

First, you need to install the WebSocket package:

```bash
npm install --save react-use-websocket
```

Then, import the `useWebSocket` hook from the package in the component where you want to use the real-time data:

```javascript
import { useWebSocket } from 'react-use-websocket';
```

In your `TradeDetails` component, you can create a function to handle the real-time data received through WebSocket:

```javascript
const handleRealTimeData = (data: any) => {
  // Process the data and update the state accordingly
};
```

After that, you can use the `useWebSocket` hook in the `TradeDetails` component to get real-time data from the Binance API, and pass the `handleRealTimeData` function as the message handler:

```javascript
const wsUrl = 'wss://stream.binance.com:9443/ws/dogeusdt@kline_' + interval;
const { sendJsonMessage, lastJsonMessage, readyState } = useWebSocket(wsUrl, {
  onMessage: handleRealTimeData,
});
```

In the `handleRealTimeData` function, you need to process the data according to your requirement and update the states, such as `candleData`, accordingly. You might need to convert the received data into the format that the charting library expects. Then you can use the real-time data to display candles on the chart and calculate the percentage at the current price.

Please note that you need to replace the `interval` variable in the `wsUrl` with the appropriate interval format supported by the Binance API, e.g., '1m' for 1 minute, '3m' for 3 minutes, etc.

Finally, to make sure the chart is updated with real-time information, you can follow a similar approach in the `CandleChart` component to update the chart when the real-time data is updated in the parent component (TradeDetails), using the `useEffect` hook.

Now your chart should display real-time candles and the percentage at the current price based on the data received from the Binance API.

To add a limited height with a red scrollbar in the DataGrid component from Material-UI, you need to update its style properties. You can pass the `sx` prop to the `DataGrid` component to provide custom styling.

Here's an example of how to set a limited height and a red scrollbar for the DataGrid component:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { DataGrid } from '@mui/x-data-grid';

const columns = [
  { field: 'id', headerName: 'ID', width: 90 },
  { field: 'name', headerName: 'Name', width: 150 },
  { field: 'age', headerName: 'Age', width: 110 },
];

const rows = [
  { id: 1, name: 'John', age: 35 },
  { id: 2, name: 'Jane', age: 42 },
  { id: 3, name: 'Jack', age: 28 },
];

export default function MyDataGrid() {
  return (
    <div style={{ width: '100%' }}>
      <DataGrid
        columns={columns}
        rows={rows}
        pageSize={4}
        rowsPerPageOptions={[4]}
        sx={{
          height: '400px',
          '& .MuiDataGrid-renderingZone': {
            scrollbarColor: 'red lightgray',
            scrollbarWidth: 'thin',
            '&::-webkit-scrollbar': {
              width: '6px',
              height: '6px',
            },
            '&::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb': {
              backgroundColor: 'red',
            },
            '&::-webkit-scrollbar-track': {
              backgroundColor: 'lightgray',
            },
          },
        }}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```

In this example, we set a limited height of '400px' to the DataGrid component, and then we apply custom styling to the scrollbar by targeting the `.MuiDataGrid-renderingZone` element. We add a red scrollbar and a light gray scrollbar track.

Now the DataGrid will have a limited height with a red scrollbar.

Here is an example Java code that demonstrates how log4j can be used to log messages in a Java application:

```java
import org.apache.log4j.Logger;

public class Example {

    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(Example.class);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        logger.debug("This is a debug message");
        logger.info("This is an info message");
        logger.warn("This is a warning message");
        logger.error("This is an error message");
        logger.fatal("This is a fatal message");
    }
}
```

This code sets up a logger object using the logger name "Example", and then logs messages at different log levels using the logger.debug, logger.info, logger.warn, logger.error, and logger.fatal methods. By default, these messages will be written to the console, but log4j can be configured to write messages to a file or other destinations as needed.

To dump messages to a file, the log4j.properties file needs to be configured with a file appender. Here's an example log4j.properties file that logs messages to a file called "example.log":

```
log4j.rootLogger=DEBUG, file

log4j.appender.file=org.apache.log4j.RollingFileAppender
log4j.appender.file.File=example.log
log4j.appender.file.MaxFileSize=10MB
log4j.appender.file.MaxBackupIndex=10
log4j.appender.file.layout=org.apache.log4j.PatternLayout
log4j.appender.file.layout.ConversionPattern=%d %p %c{1} - %m%n
```

This configuration sets the root logger's log level to DEBUG and adds a file appender that will write messages to a file called "example.log". The RollingFileAppender is used, which will rotate the log file when it reaches a certain size (in this case, 10 megabytes) and keep up to 10 backups of old log files. The PatternLayout is used to format the log messages, which includes the date, log level, logger name, and message.

With this configuration, any log messages sent to the root logger will be written to the "example.log" file. For example, the following Java code would log messages to the file:

```java
import org.apache.log4j.Logger;

public class Example {

    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(Example.class);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        logger.debug("This is a debug message");
        logger.info("This is an info message");
        logger.warn("This is a warning message");
        logger.error("This is an error message");
        logger.fatal("This is a fatal message");
    }
}
```

When this code is executed with the log4j.properties file in the classpath, the log messages will be written to the "example.log" file instead of the console.

